&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Catherine Cusset&lt;/strong&gt; in conversation with Elisabeth Ladenson&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Register &lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/how-proust-and-hockney-can-lift-your-spirits-tickets-1425808825579?aff=oddtdtcreator"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One has a reputation for being a difficult writer; the other is an easy painter, accessible to all. Catherine Cusset has devoted a novel to the British David Hockney and a personal essay to Marcel Proust. In their work she finds a life lesson, inspiration, comfort, irony, and a certainty: that art is a life saver, and that you should only write, or paint, what matters to you.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Catherine Cusset&lt;/strong&gt; is an award-winning, bestselling author of 15 novels, including &lt;em&gt;Le probl&amp;egrave;me avec Jane&lt;/em&gt; (Grand Prix des lectrices d&amp;rsquo;Elle 2000); &lt;em&gt;Confessions d&amp;rsquo;une radine&lt;/em&gt;; &lt;em&gt;Un brillant avenir&lt;/em&gt; (Prix Goncourt des Lyc&amp;eacute;ens 2008),&lt;em&gt; L&amp;rsquo;autre qu&amp;rsquo;on adorait&lt;/em&gt; (Finalist for the Prix Goncourt 2016), &lt;em&gt;Vie de David Hockney&lt;/em&gt; (Prix Ana&amp;iuml;s Nin 2018) and&lt;em&gt; La d&amp;eacute;finition du bonheur&lt;/em&gt;. Her most recent book,&lt;em&gt; Ma vie avec Marcel Proust&lt;/em&gt; (&lt;em&gt;My Life with Marcel Proust&lt;/em&gt;), in 2025, was awarded the L&amp;eacute;o Scheer and C&amp;eacute;leste Albaret prizes, and her novel on David Hockney was republished in an illustrated version with 50 paintings by the British artist.&lt;/p&gt;

